In one of the integration scenarios I worked on in SAP CPI, I designed an iFlow that handles booking
confirmations. The use case is as follows:

Step 1: HTTP Sender Adapter
The iFlow is triggered by an incoming XML payload from a third-party system through the HTTP sender adapter.
This XML contains booking details such as the booking ID, customer information, Request dates, Delivery date
waybill number and destination.

Step 2: XSLT Mapping
The received XML data is then transformed into an HTML format using an XSLT mapping. This transformation
is performed to generate a user-friendly email content layout with a well-structured table that displays
all the booking information clearly.

Step 3: Mail Receiver Adapter
After the HTML content is generated, it is passed to the Mail receiver adapter. I configured the mail
adapter to send the HTML email to both the person who made the booking and the recipient
(if it is a gift or booked for someone else).

Additional Configurations:
The content type is set to text/html to ensure the email client renders the HTML properly.
The subject and recipients are dynamically set using properties or content modifiers based on the input data.
This iFlow demonstrates the use of XSLT for HTML generation, dynamic mail configurations, and seamless integration
between external systems and email communication via SAP CPI.

